  <steps mode="directional">
    <step order="0">Hand-trace the interleaving against the code before writing anything, and confirm the SHORTENING outcome is reachable at HEAD (the register's scenario, against `:204` read → `:215` elect → `:260` write → `:321` collapse). Then choose the mechanism and answer the escalation: optional precondition on the shared client versus a narrower fix, and what the endpoint returns on a detected conflict.</step>
    <step order="1">Implement the detection. If the precondition path is chosen: read the row version in `RowSelect`/`ExternalGrantRow`, thread it through `QueryActiveRowsAsync` and `DeactivateAsync`, add ADDITIVE precondition support to `DataverseWebApiClient.UpdateAsync` defaulting to current behaviour, and map a 412 to a distinguishable result rather than a bare transport exception.</step>
    <step order="2">Decide and implement the endpoint's response on conflict — retry once on a fresh read, or refuse with a stable reason code. Whichever: the collapse must never deactivate a row whose state changed since the read, and the swallowing catch must not silently absorb the conflict.</step>
    <step order="3">Cover the revoke-race half, or record it as out of scope with a reason. Check the ripple on `RevokeExternalAccessEndpoint.cs:152`.</step>
    <step order="4">Tests per the closed set — each spanning TWO interleaved writers, not one request. Perturbations. Build, full suite, ArchTests, publish size versus a fresh master worktree; notes; report. Leave TASK-INDEX to the main session.</step>
  </steps>

  <escalation>
    <trigger>The MECHANISM is a judgment call with a shared-library consequence the register does not mention. `If-Match` closes the window but requires additive precondition support on `DataverseWebApiClient` (used by every BFF write path) plus a row-version field and 412 handling; a re-read immediately before the collapse narrows the window without closing it and touches nothing shared. Present both with the blast radius and a recommendation before implementing. Do not start editing the shared client on your own judgment.</trigger>
    <trigger>If detection is achievable but the correct RESPONSE is unclear — retry-once versus refuse — stop and ask. A silent retry that lands on a different survivor is a new request-dependence in disguise, which constraint "task 106" forbids; a refusal changes the endpoint's contract for a case callers have never seen.</trigger>
  </escalation>

  <justification>
    <existing>`DataverseWebApiClient.UpdateAsync` (`:204-214`) — a PATCH with no precondition support; `ExternalGrantRow` (`:78-120`) — no row-version field; `DeactivateAsync` (`:406-422`) — an unconditional statecode write.</existing>
    <extension>Extends the EXISTING `UpdateAsync` with an optional precondition and the EXISTING row model with the version it already receives from Dataverse but discards. No new client, no second write path, no package.</extension>
    <cost-of-doing-nothing>A concurrent `/set-record-share-expiry` during a date-less re-grant collapses the key onto a row that is now shorter and deactivates the longer one: conferred access silently drops from today+10 to today+1 while both callers receive 200. Symmetrically, a concurrent revoke of the elected row leaves the key with ZERO active rows and still returns 200. Neither is detectable after the fact — the rows simply read as correct — and no existing test can see either, because every instrument on this path is single-request.</cost-of-doing-nothing>
  </justification>

