  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion testable="true">The shortening interleaving is pinned by a test that spans two writers: rows at today+10 (lower id) and today+200, a date-less grant that reads both, the elected row's expiry moved to today+1 between the read and the collapse — and the outcome is NOT "the longer row deactivated while the caller receives 200".</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">On a detected conflict the endpoint's behaviour matches the escalation answer exactly (retry-once on a fresh read, or refuse with a stable reason code), and a test asserts it.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">The collapse never deactivates a row whose state changed since the read that elected the survivor.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Negative case — no concurrency: a grant over a key with duplicates and no competing writer still collapses exactly as it does today, with the same survivor, the same level and expiry writes, and the same 200.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">The ADR-003 expired-survivor 409 (`:306-319`) still fires on the same inputs and still speaks for the whole key.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Request-independence is intact: `ElectSurvivor` still takes no request value, and the existing test that the same survivor is elected whether or not the request carries an expiry (`GrantLifecycleCharacterizationTests.cs:1089`) stays green.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">The revoke-race half is either covered by a test (a racer deactivates the elected row; the key does not end with zero active rows reported as success) or explicitly recorded as out of scope with a reason in the notes.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">No conflict is silently swallowed: the `CollapseDuplicatesAsync` catch (`:480-485`) does not absorb a precondition failure into a logged warning that reports success to the caller.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">If the shared client changed: the precondition is OPTIONAL and every existing caller's behaviour is byte-identical without it, `RevokeExternalAccessEndpoint.cs:152` still compiles and behaves as before, and the existing revoke tests stay green.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Perturbation: removing the precondition (or the re-read) fails at least one test; and reintroducing a request value into the election fails at least one test.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Build green; full suite green; ArchTests green; publish size reported against a fresh master worktree and ≤60 MB; no new HIGH CVE.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Cover the stated contract plus the edge cases named above. Anything beyond that needs a one-line justification in task notes.</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>

  <notes>Authored 2026-09-19. Premises re-verified against HEAD. The DEFECT is real and reachable; the register's FIX was under-scoped.

**VERIFIED TRUE** — read→elect→write→collapse with no concurrency control of any kind (`GrantExternalAccessEndpoint.cs:204`, `:215`, `:260`, `:321`; `ExternalGrantLifecycle.DeactivateAsync:416`). The election key IS the mutable `sprk_expiresdate`, read at T₀ (`ConferralRank:217-218`, `RowSelect:261-264`, ordering `:252-255`). `ElectSurvivor` (`:245`) takes no request value — request-independence is structural. The concurrent writer exists and writes ONLY `sprk_expiresdate` (`SetRecordShareExpiryEndpoint.cs:213-216`).

**VERIFIED UNDER-SCOPED — the suggested fix.** `defer-issues.md:729-731` proposes "`If-Match` on the row's ETag … the read-then-write pair currently has no optimistic concurrency at all". The second half is exactly right. The first half is not a one-line change: `ExternalGrantRow` has NO etag property (`:78-120`), `RowSelect` never asks for one (`:261-264`), `DataverseWebApiClient.UpdateAsync` has no header hook (`:204-214`, request built at `:161-167`), there is no If-Match support anywhere in that client, and `EnsureSuccessStatusCode()` turns a 412 into a bare `HttpRequestException`. The fix therefore reaches `src/server/shared/Spaarke.Dataverse`, which is why this task was re-tiered to opus and marked parallel-unsafe beyond `Api/ExternalAccess/**`.

**Also verified, and not in the register**: `DeactivateAsync` has a second caller (`RevokeExternalAccessEndpoint.cs:152`), so a required-precondition signature changes the revoke path too. And the two racing writers use different clients — Web API PATCH for the collapse, SDK `BulkUpdateAsync` for the expiry setter (`DataverseServiceClientImpl.cs:2327`) — so they share no transaction; a row-version precondition detects the other writer but nothing can make them coordinate.

Existing coverage, checked: `GrantLifecycleCharacterizationTests.cs` has the request-independence test (`:1089`) and no concurrency test; `RecordShareExpiryTests.cs` has eighteen tests and none interleaves a second writer. Per plan §7 this is one of three queued items whose defect lives BETWEEN requests — a green suite is not evidence.

Related: tasks 010 (convergence semantics), 023 (the match path's expiry write), 098 (write-path row reads), 106 (the election), 113 (ISS-023, the LEVEL half of the same collapse — coordinate if both are in flight).</notes>

